You have great debugging instincts. If the frontend is correctly looking for the error but still getting a generic message, **the backend's Global Exception Handler is swallowing our specific message and returning a generic one.**
When VoucherBulkValidationService throws the FileProcessingException("File name length cannot exceed 20 characters."), it bubbles up to your Controller. If the Controller doesn't handle it, Spring Boot sends it to your Global @ControllerAdvice, which is likely overriding our specific message with the generic *"Failed to process the uploaded file."*
We can easily fix this by catching the exception explicitly inside the **VoucherBulkUploadController** and returning it directly to the frontend as a clean JSON object, bypassing the generic global handler.
Open **VoucherBulkUploadController.java** and replace your initiateValidation method with this updated version:
```java
    @PostMapping(value = "/bulk-validate-init", consumes = MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE)
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> initiateValidation(
            @RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file,
            @RequestParam("postingDate") @DateTimeFormat(pattern = "dd/MM/yyyy") LocalDate postingDate,
            HttpServletRequest request) {

        if (file == null || file.isEmpty()) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(org.springframework.http.HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
                    .body(Map.of("message", "File is missing or empty."));
        }

        try {
            String reqId = voucherBulkValidationService.initiateValidation(file, postingDate);
            
            HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
            headers.setCacheControl(org.springframework.http.CacheControl.noCache().mustRevalidate().sMaxAge(java.time.Duration.ZERO));
            headers.setPragma("no-cache"); 
            headers.setExpires(0L); 
            headers.set("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff"); 
            headers.set("Content-Security-Policy", "default-src 'self'");
            
            return ResponseEntity.ok()
                    .headers(headers)
                    .body(Map.of("status", "QUEUED", "requestId", reqId));

        } catch (CustomExceptions.FileProcessingException e) {
            // FIX: Explicitly catch our custom validation errors and send the EXACT message back to React
            return ResponseEntity.status(org.springframework.http.HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
                    .body(Map.of("message", e.getMessage()));
                    
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Fallback for unexpected system errors
            log.error("VALIDATION_INIT_FAIL | File: {} | Error: {}", file.getOriginalFilename(), e.getMessage());
            return ResponseEntity.status(org.springframework.http.HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
                    .body(Map.of("message", "An unexpected error occurred during upload."));
        }
    }

```
### Why this fixes it completely:
Instead of throwing the error back to Spring Boot, we are catching it on the spot and safely packing it into a 400 Bad Request with Map.of("message", e.getMessage()).
Because we formatted it specifically as {"message": "File name length cannot exceed 20 characters."}, your updated React catch block will extract it flawlessly via err.response.data.message and display it in the Snackbar! Restart the backend and test the long filename again!
